#1 Examples and output

Each example labels its source language to clarify the difference between `.sm` input, HTML output and structured data. The examples use StructMark's grammar, not an unrelated `@[]` format.

#2 From `.sm` to HTML

```sm
#1 Hello (#hello)

This is a *[structured] document.
```

```html
<h1 id="hello">Hello</h1>
<p>This is a <strong>structured</strong> document.</p>
```

#2 What semantic blocks mean

```sm
&note[title="Note"]
Additional context.
&end

&warning[title="Warning"]
Conversion may affect the result.
&end
```

#2 TypeScript API

```typescript
import {parseDocument, renderHtml} from "structmark-language-core";

const source = "#1 Hello\n\n*[Strong] text.";
const document = parseDocument(source);
const html = renderHtml(document);
```

#2 Syntax diagnostics

The parsed result contains source-located diagnostics. Invalid input should show the offending line and code rather than an empty preview.

```sm
&note[title="Not closed"]
Missing closing directive.
```

#2 Try in Playground

Open `.sm` examples directly in Playground. Existing drafts are confirmed before replacement.

```sm
#1 Text and meaning

*[Bold] and /[italic].

&note[title="Note"]
This is a semantic directive, not merely a color.
&end
```
